@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Block Out a Courtyard") {
        In this series, you build a small courtyard level with Xogot Modeler. You
        do not need 3D modeling experience.

        In this first tutorial, you draw the floor, the walls, a platform, pillars,
        and a flower bed. You use only primitive shapes.

        Every tool in this series has a full description in the reference page
        <doc:/documentation/Xogot/Xogot-Modeler>. This tutorial uses <doc:/documentation/Xogot/Xogot-Modeler#Add-a-mesh>,
        <doc:/documentation/Xogot/Xogot-Modeler#Draw-a-shape>, <doc:/documentation/Xogot/Xogot-Modeler#Draw-a-Poly-Shape>, and
        <doc:/documentation/Xogot/Xogot-Modeler#Move-the-camera>.

        @Image(source: "modeler-01-intro-blockout.png",
               alt: "The blocked-out courtyard: a floor, four walls, a platform, pillars, and a flower bed")
    }

    @Section(title: "Prepare the Scene") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Modeler works in the 3D editor. Start with an empty 3D scene.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-empty-scene.png",
                   alt: "A new 3D scene with a Node3D root named Courtyard")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a new scene. Select **3D Scene** as the root node.

                Rename the root node to `Courtyard`. Save the scene as
                `courtyard.tscn`.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-new-3d-scene.png",
                       alt: "The Scene tree with a Node3D root named Courtyard")
            }

            @Step {
                Find **Add Mesh** in the 3D toolbar, and open it.

                The palette shows the 12 shapes that Modeler can create.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-add-mesh-palette.png",
                       alt: "The Add Mesh palette open in the 3D toolbar, showing the twelve shape tiles")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Draw the Floor") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            To draw a shape, you tap its tile. Then you draw the base of the shape
            on the grid.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-floor-done.png",
                   alt: "A flat Plane that covers the courtyard floor")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Tap **Plane** in the palette.

                The palette closes, and the viewport HUD shows the name of the shape
                and the current step.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-plane-armed-hud.png",
                       alt: "The viewport HUD for the Plane tool, waiting for the first point")
            }

            @Step {
                Hold Command (macOS) or Ctrl, then press and drag on the grid to draw
                a 20 × 20 base. The HUD shows the dimensions.

                On a touch screen, turn on **Snap** in the HUD, and drag with one
                finger.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-plane-base-drag.gif",
                       alt: "A Plane base drawn on the grid while the HUD shows its dimensions")
            }

            @Step {
                Release.

                A Plane has no height, so the shape is complete when you release. Xogot
                selects the new mesh.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-plane-selected.png",
                       alt: "The completed floor Plane, selected in the viewport and in the Scene tree")
            }

            @Step {
                Rename the new node to `Floor`.

                > Tip: Give each shape a clear name. A level with many shapes is
                > easier to edit when you can find each node in the Scene tree.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-floor-renamed.png",
                       alt: "The Scene tree with a node named Floor under Courtyard")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Draw the Walls") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A Cube needs two steps. First you draw the base, and then you set the
            height.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-four-walls.png",
                   alt: "Four walls around the courtyard floor")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open **Add Mesh** and tap **Cube**.

                Hold Command or Ctrl, and draw a long, thin base along one edge of the
                floor. Make it 20 units long and 1 unit wide.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-wall-base.png",
                       alt: "A long, thin Cube base along the edge of the floor")
            }

            @Step {
                Release. Then move the pointer up to set the height of the wall to 4
                units.

                The preview shows the wall before you complete it. The HUD shows the
                height.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-wall-height.gif",
                       alt: "The wall height set by moving the pointer up, with the HUD in the Height phase")
            }

            @Step {
                Hold Shift, and click or tap to complete the wall.

                Because you held Shift, the Cube tool stays active with the same
                settings.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-wall-shift-repeat.png",
                       alt: "The first wall complete, with the Cube tool still active for the next wall")
            }

            @Step {
                Draw the three other walls in the same way. Complete the last wall
                without Shift, or select **Done**.

                Leave a 4-unit gap in the front wall for a gate.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-walls-with-gap.png",
                       alt: "Four walls around the floor, with a gap in the front wall")
            }

            @Step {
                If you make a mistake, press Escape or select **Cancel** before you
                complete the shape.

                After you complete a shape, use Undo. One Undo removes the complete
                shape.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-hud-cancel.png",
                       alt: "The Cancel and Done buttons in the viewport HUD")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Build a Platform") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Modeler draws on the surface under the pointer. This lets you stack
            shapes on top of other shapes.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-platform.png",
                   alt: "A raised platform at the back of the courtyard")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Tap **Cube**. Move the pointer over the floor, near the back wall.

                The placement cursor sits on the floor surface.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-surface-cursor.png",
                       alt: "The placement cursor on the floor surface")
            }

            @Step {
                Draw an 8 × 4 base, and set the height to 1.5 units. Complete the
                shape. Rename it to `Platform`.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-platform-height.png",
                       alt: "The platform Cube in the Height phase")
            }

            @Step {
                You can also draw above empty space. Tap **Cube**, and before you start
                the base, press `]` two times.

                The drawing plane moves up to Y = 2. The HUD shows the Y value. Press
                `[` to move it down. Press Escape to cancel this shape.

                > Note: The bracket keys are for a keyboard. On a touch screen, draw
                > on an existing surface at the height that you need.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-drawing-plane-y.png",
                       alt: "The HUD showing the drawing plane at Y = 2")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Place Pillars Immediately") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            You do not always have to draw a shape. You can place a shape at the
            center of the view with one gesture.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-pillars.png",
                   alt: "Four cylinder pillars at the corners of the platform")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Orbit the camera so that the center of the view is on a corner of the
                platform.

                The camera orbits around its focus point. Modeler places immediate
                shapes at this point.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-focus-point.png",
                       alt: "The view centered on a corner of the platform")
            }

            @Step {
                Open **Add Mesh**. Option-tap or Alt-tap **Cylinder**. On a touch
                screen, touch and hold the **Cylinder** tile.

                Xogot creates the cylinder at the focus point. The palette now marks
                Cylinder as the last-used shape.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-cylinder-immediate.png",
                       alt: "A Cylinder created at the focus point, with Cylinder marked in the palette")
            }

            @Step {
                Move the view to each of the other corners, and press
                **Option-Shift-K**.

                Each time, Xogot creates the last-used shape at the focus point. You
                change the size of the pillars in the next tutorial.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-option-shift-k.png",
                       alt: "Four pillars created with Option-Shift-K")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Draw a Flower Bed") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A Poly Shape is a mesh made from an outline that you draw. It can have
            holes. Use it to make a raised flower bed.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-flower-bed.png",
                   alt: "A raised flower bed with a hole in the middle for soil")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Start **Poly Shape**. Click or tap on the floor to add six points of an
                outline.

                A preview line connects the points.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-poly-outline.png",
                       alt: "A six-point Poly Shape outline on the floor")
            }

            @Step {
                Click or tap the first point to close the outline.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-poly-closed.png",
                       alt: "The closed Poly Shape outline")
            }

            @Step {
                Select **Add Hole**. Add four points inside the outline, and click or tap
                the first point of the hole to close it.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-poly-hole.png",
                       alt: "A hole drawn inside the Poly Shape outline")
            }

            @Step {
                Move the pointer up to set a height of 0.5 units. Click or press Return
                to create the mesh.

                The tool stays active, so you can draw more outlines. Press Escape when
                you are done.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-poly-height.png",
                       alt: "The flower bed Poly Shape with its height set")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Move Around the Courtyard") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Use the camera to look at your work from all sides. Camera gestures also
            work while a Modeler tool is active.

            @Image(source: "modeler-01-courtyard-overview.png",
                   alt: "The blocked-out courtyard seen from above")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                With a mouse or trackpad: drag on empty space to orbit, drag with the
                middle button or scroll with two fingers to pan, and use the mouse
                wheel or pinch to zoom.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-camera-mouse.gif",
                       alt: "The camera orbiting the courtyard with the mouse")
            }

            @Step {
                On a touch screen: drag with one finger on empty space to orbit, drag
                with two fingers to pan, and pinch to zoom.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-camera-touch.gif",
                       alt: "The camera orbiting the courtyard with touch gestures")
            }

            @Step {
                Save the scene.

                In the next tutorial, you change the size and settings of these shapes,
                and you add a gate, an arch, stairs, and a fountain.

                @Image(source: "modeler-01-saved.png",
                       alt: "The saved courtyard scene")
            }
        }
    }
}
